# dir-mcp Configuration

When the user asks to configure dir-mcp — change the server address, set an auth token, switch auth mode, configure OIDC or TLS, or update the OASF schema URL — follow the `configure-dir-mcp` skill.

## No config file

dir-mcp keeps no config file of its own. The MCP server reads `DIRECTORY_CLIENT_*` (and `OASF_API_VALIDATION_SCHEMA_URL`) environment variables directly, set in the `agntcy-dir` server's `env` block inside `~/.cursor/mcp.json` (global) or `.cursor/mcp.json` (per-project).

The bundled `dirctl` binary additionally has its own, separate config with named contexts at `~/.config/dirctl/config.yaml` — but that only affects direct `dirctl` invocations (e.g. `dirctl auth login`), never the MCP server's own tool calls. Don't conflate the two; see the `configure-dir-mcp` skill for the distinction.

## Precedence

`DIRECTORY_CLIENT_*` env vars set in `mcp.json`'s `env` block also override `dirctl`'s selected context when `dirctl` is invoked directly, so keeping connection settings in that one place covers both.

## After changes

Unlike the old config-file design, there's no watch-and-restart-on-change. After editing `mcp.json`'s `env` block, reload the MCP server (Cursor's MCP panel, or restart Cursor) for the change to take effect.
